WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.222 --> 00:00:11.430
KURT ENZINGER: Hey, everyone.

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:15.230
Welcome to "Cloud OnAir," live
webinars from Google Cloud.

00:00:15.230 --> 00:00:16.730
We've been hosting
these webinars

00:00:16.730 --> 00:00:18.500
every Tuesday this February.

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:22.790
My name's Kurt Enzinger, and
I am a Google Cloud specialist

00:00:22.790 --> 00:00:26.480
that specializes in our
productivity and collaboration

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:27.410
tools.

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:30.200
This afternoon, I'm going to
be spending some time talking

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:32.450
with you about different
tips and tricks

00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:35.000
to get the most out of G Suite.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.160
You can ask questions
anytime using the platform,

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:39.890
and we have Googlers standing
on by to help answer them.

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:41.360
Sound good?

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:43.370
All right.

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:46.670
So the first stop in
our tour today, we're

00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:49.630
going to be talking about Gmail.

00:00:49.630 --> 00:00:53.920
As you are all aware, Gmail
is a pretty good application.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:56.680
And you're probably pretty
familiar with how to use it.

00:00:56.680 --> 00:00:58.780
But the first thing that
I'm going to show you

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:00.710
is the Search feature.

00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:03.700
So I think you all are
familiar with the fact

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:05.200
that you can type
in a search term

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:08.622
or you can type in an
email and spend time

00:01:08.622 --> 00:01:09.830
looking through all of those.

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:11.496
But what I want to
do is sort of uplevel

00:01:11.496 --> 00:01:13.060
your ability to do
Google searches.

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:17.050
So we're going to be working in
a domain today called Onsited.

00:01:17.050 --> 00:01:20.290
And in this domain, I've
subscribed to a lot of rock

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:24.280
climbing and mountaineering
different newsletters.

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:28.300
So let's say we're going to
look for a term called "belay."

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:30.730
We've been able to pull up all
these different emails that

00:01:30.730 --> 00:01:32.600
have "belay"
associated with them,

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:34.750
but I need to do a little
bit more searching.

00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:37.510
I want to dig in a
little bit on this.

00:01:37.510 --> 00:01:40.630
If we come over here, this
is what we call our Advanced

00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:41.710
Search options.

00:01:41.710 --> 00:01:43.840
We can click down
into Advanced Search,

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:45.670
and we see a whole
other interface of ways

00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:49.720
to get more granular in the type
of searching that we're doing.

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:51.430
First thing that
we'll see is we can

00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:54.670
choose the location that we're
going to be doing our search.

00:01:54.670 --> 00:01:56.770
We can choose All Mail, Inbox.

00:01:56.770 --> 00:02:00.120
In this case, I'm going
to look for Unread Mail.

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:03.390
We have the ability to add a
From field that will tell us

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:05.310
who sent the email,
we have the ability

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:08.130
to search by who the
email was sent to,

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:10.650
as well as keywords
in the subject,

00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:12.900
and also keywords
anywhere in the email.

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:17.020
In this case, we brought "belay"
over from our original search.

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:19.080
You can also specify
words that you don't want

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:20.790
to be included in the message.

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:24.160
So for example, we don't want to
talk about ice climbing today.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:25.770
So we're going to remove that.

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:27.750
And then you have
options to determine

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:31.570
if there is attachments or
if there are chats available,

00:02:31.570 --> 00:02:33.490
as well as size and date.

00:02:33.490 --> 00:02:35.700
So we'll click
through this search

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:38.760
and we've gone from
23 different emails

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:40.830
down to three, which is
much more manageable.

00:02:40.830 --> 00:02:44.580
It gives you the ability
to look through and use

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:46.630
your emails more effectively.

00:02:46.630 --> 00:02:49.030
Now, that's pretty cool.

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:52.380
But I think that many
of you are probably

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:55.080
going to want to be what we call
power users of Gmail, right?

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:58.110
So here's what I would
recommend that you do if you'd

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:00.650
like to be a Gmail power user.

00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:05.750
Open up and go to Google.com
and search for the term

00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:09.720
"Gmail search operators."

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:13.890
The first hit is a help article
from our Gmail Help pages.

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:16.920
Bookmark this and then do your
best to try and memorize these.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:20.670
There's a whole long
list of search operators

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:24.330
that you can use within
Gmail to try and do

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:25.680
more granular searches.

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:30.060
So for example, if we
come back to our inbox,

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:34.650
let's say we want to see any
emails that are unread that

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:40.650
have been sent before
January 1 of this year

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:42.222
and has an attachment.

00:03:45.530 --> 00:03:48.530
Just with a simple set
of search operators,

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:51.980
we're able to get very
specific on the types of emails

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:54.230
that we're pulling up to
search for, which gives you

00:03:54.230 --> 00:03:56.170
a lot of power in using Gmail.

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:00.324
Awesome.

00:04:00.324 --> 00:04:02.490
So we've learned a little
bit about Advanced Search.

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:04.890
We've learned a little bit
about search operators.

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:07.897
But let's think of another
way that we can get organized.

00:04:07.897 --> 00:04:09.480
And for that, I want
to share with you

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:12.270
some information about labels.

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:17.490
A label is something that can
be approximated as a folder,

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:19.720
but with some key differences.

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:23.490
Essentially, a label in
Gmail is a different view

00:04:23.490 --> 00:04:26.160
of your mail environment
that allows you

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:29.960
to see different sets of data.

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:32.550
Inbox, Starred, Sent
Mail, and Drafts-- those

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:35.730
are all examples
of default labels.

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:39.600
But we have the ability
to create our own labels.

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:41.790
So if you notice,
over here we've

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:45.420
got a lot of messages coming
from "Rock and Ice Magazine."

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:47.640
And since my boss
doesn't really like it

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:50.342
when I'm looking at rock
climbing magazines on the job,

00:04:50.342 --> 00:04:52.050
we're going to filter
these out and we're

00:04:52.050 --> 00:04:54.780
going to label them so that
they're out of our way.

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:57.395
So we'll create a
new label, and we're

00:04:57.395 --> 00:05:01.200
going to call this Rock and Ice.

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:03.630
If you notice here, we can
nest this label underneath

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:04.992
of another one.

00:05:04.992 --> 00:05:07.200
We could put it under
Newsletters, Personal, anything

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:07.699
we want.

00:05:07.699 --> 00:05:09.990
But just for the
sake of ease, we're

00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:12.450
going to create its
own top-level label.

00:05:12.450 --> 00:05:15.600
And notice, now, over
here on the right

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:17.700
is a label called Rock and Ice.

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:19.860
If we click on it
right now, there's

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:20.910
no conversations in here.

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:22.920
We haven't labeled anything.

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:25.090
Nothing really has happened yet.

00:05:25.090 --> 00:05:27.930
So we'll come back to
our top-level inbox.

00:05:27.930 --> 00:05:29.790
And there's a couple
of different ways,

00:05:29.790 --> 00:05:32.670
now, that we can
interact with this label.

00:05:32.670 --> 00:05:35.970
The first would be
to take a message

00:05:35.970 --> 00:05:39.270
and drag it and drop
it over into our label.

00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:40.920
That message has
now been archived

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:43.540
and it has the label Rock
and Ice applied to it.

00:05:43.540 --> 00:05:46.752
So if we click in there,
here's our Rock and Ice.

00:05:46.752 --> 00:05:48.210
We can also go the
other direction.

00:05:48.210 --> 00:05:51.900
So let's say we can
select a few of these,

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:54.580
and now we want to label
these with Rock and Ice,

00:05:54.580 --> 00:05:56.400
but not archive them.

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:00.970
We take Rock and Ice from our
label set, we drop it on top,

00:06:00.970 --> 00:06:04.560
and now they're all
labeled Rock and Ice.

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:06.030
The interesting
thing about these--

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:07.860
now, if you click
on one of these

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:10.820
and into one of these emails,
you can see up at the top

00:06:10.820 --> 00:06:12.310
that it has the
label Rock and Ice

00:06:12.310 --> 00:06:14.520
and it also has the
label Inbox because it

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:16.980
lives in both of those worlds.

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:19.550
To make labels even
more useful, we

00:06:19.550 --> 00:06:22.544
can actually adjust the color.

00:06:22.544 --> 00:06:23.960
This is going to
help it stand out

00:06:23.960 --> 00:06:25.410
and make it easier
for you to see.

00:06:25.410 --> 00:06:29.510
So in this case, we'll turn
this label into bright orange.

00:06:29.510 --> 00:06:31.250
There it is-- orange up there.

00:06:31.250 --> 00:06:32.940
It's orange down here.

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:34.790
And if we come back
into our inbox,

00:06:34.790 --> 00:06:39.770
here are all the Rock
and Ice labeled items.

00:06:39.770 --> 00:06:41.360
That's all well and good.

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:44.150
I've taught you how to create a
label, how to change the color,

00:06:44.150 --> 00:06:45.410
how to drag and drop things.

00:06:45.410 --> 00:06:48.860
But you probably don't want
to spend your entire day

00:06:48.860 --> 00:06:51.590
dragging and dropping emails
back and forth, right?

00:06:51.590 --> 00:06:54.350
Well, good news-- we're
going to use something

00:06:54.350 --> 00:06:58.250
called a filter to try and
help automate this process.

00:06:58.250 --> 00:07:01.130
A filter is going to
start at the same place

00:07:01.130 --> 00:07:02.990
that the Advanced Search does.

00:07:02.990 --> 00:07:04.850
And in this case, we're
going to filter out

00:07:04.850 --> 00:07:08.040
the emails that have the
words "Rock and Ice"--

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:11.250
so just the words "Rock
and Ice" and nothing else.

00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:13.380
So we're going to
put in a quotation.

00:07:13.380 --> 00:07:17.564
We'll type "Rock and
Ice," close quotation.

00:07:17.564 --> 00:07:18.980
This is going to
ensure that we're

00:07:18.980 --> 00:07:21.440
looking for just
that phrase and not

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:24.319
for any email that
has the word "Rock"

00:07:24.319 --> 00:07:26.360
or any email that has the
word "Ice" because that

00:07:26.360 --> 00:07:30.210
could complicate things a bit.

00:07:30.210 --> 00:07:32.550
What we'll do from here,
before I create the filter,

00:07:32.550 --> 00:07:36.300
is I actually just go through
with the search and take a look

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:38.040
and see to make
sure that I'm not

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:40.200
picking up anything unusual.

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:41.550
Everything looks good.

00:07:41.550 --> 00:07:43.650
So we'll come back
in and we're going

00:07:43.650 --> 00:07:48.300
to create a filter
with this search.

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:52.552
Now that we're in the
filter options here,

00:07:52.552 --> 00:07:54.510
there's a lot of things
that we can do with it.

00:07:54.510 --> 00:07:57.180
We can skip the inbox,
which we'll do in this case.

00:07:57.180 --> 00:07:59.340
You can mark it as read so
that you don't even have

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:01.200
to realize that it came in.

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:03.720
I want to read it at some
point, so I'll leave it.

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:05.190
You can star it.

00:08:05.190 --> 00:08:06.231
You can apply the label.

00:08:06.231 --> 00:08:07.730
That's what we're
going to do today.

00:08:07.730 --> 00:08:09.645
We're going to apply
the label Rock and Ice.

00:08:09.645 --> 00:08:11.270
Then you can do things
like forward it,

00:08:11.270 --> 00:08:14.670
delete it, send it to
spam, make sure it's always

00:08:14.670 --> 00:08:17.550
marked important, all
kinds of good stuff there.

00:08:17.550 --> 00:08:19.900
And you would hit
Create the Filter.

00:08:19.900 --> 00:08:23.040
Now, once we hit Create
Filter, any new email

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:26.760
that matches this search
will apply these rules.

00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:28.440
It will skip the
inbox it, it will

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:31.750
apply the label Rock and Ice,
and it will be good to go.

00:08:31.750 --> 00:08:34.080
But what about all
these existing ones

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:35.610
that are already there?

00:08:35.610 --> 00:08:39.240
I can just simply check
this box right here, Also

00:08:39.240 --> 00:08:42.150
Apply Filter to
Matching Conversations,

00:08:42.150 --> 00:08:46.200
we create the filter,
and now all of those

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:50.590
are filtered out
into Rock and Ice.

00:08:50.590 --> 00:08:51.550
Nice and easy, right?

00:08:51.550 --> 00:08:54.850
Gives you an automatic
way to manage your inbox,

00:08:54.850 --> 00:08:58.750
to organize your labels, and
we've also learned a little bit

00:08:58.750 --> 00:09:02.160
about how to do some searching.

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:03.000
Awesome.

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:05.130
Well, the next thing
that we want to do,

00:09:05.130 --> 00:09:06.838
now that we've talked
about ways that you

00:09:06.838 --> 00:09:08.970
can sort of automatically
organize your emails,

00:09:08.970 --> 00:09:11.460
I like to spend time with
our customers telling them

00:09:11.460 --> 00:09:14.680
about how to visually
organize your inbox.

00:09:14.680 --> 00:09:17.110
There's a lot of
different ways to do that.

00:09:17.110 --> 00:09:19.350
The first thing that
I like to show people

00:09:19.350 --> 00:09:22.020
is turning on and off
Conversation View.

00:09:22.020 --> 00:09:26.520
I've been a Gmail user since
it was in beta many, many years

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:29.130
ago, so I love
Conversation View.

00:09:29.130 --> 00:09:32.040
But it's what I would
call a polarizing setting.

00:09:32.040 --> 00:09:34.050
Either you love
it or you hate it,

00:09:34.050 --> 00:09:35.610
and I want to give
you the ability

00:09:35.610 --> 00:09:39.750
to turn it on and off
depending on what suits you.

00:09:39.750 --> 00:09:41.850
A conversation in Google,
just for those of you

00:09:41.850 --> 00:09:43.890
that may not be
aware, is essentially

00:09:43.890 --> 00:09:46.530
where we take messages that
are related together, usually

00:09:46.530 --> 00:09:49.540
by subject line, and we
bundle them together.

00:09:49.540 --> 00:09:52.590
So here, at the top, is an
example of a conversation.

00:09:52.590 --> 00:09:54.420
It might be a little
confusing because I

00:09:54.420 --> 00:09:57.810
had the conversation with myself
for demonstration purposes,

00:09:57.810 --> 00:09:59.430
but we can click
into here and we

00:09:59.430 --> 00:10:02.400
can see that there are
three different messages.

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:05.460
First is the message that
I sent, then a reply,

00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:07.800
and then another reply there.

00:10:07.800 --> 00:10:12.540
If you want to debundle these so
that they don't exist together,

00:10:12.540 --> 00:10:15.030
you simply come
into your Settings

00:10:15.030 --> 00:10:17.760
and you go into Settings again.

00:10:17.760 --> 00:10:21.030
Now, at the top, you'll notice
there are a number of tabs

00:10:21.030 --> 00:10:22.440
for your settings.

00:10:22.440 --> 00:10:25.930
This one's going to live
in the General area.

00:10:25.930 --> 00:10:29.010
About one third of the way
down is Conversation View.

00:10:29.010 --> 00:10:31.450
You turn Conversation View off.

00:10:31.450 --> 00:10:32.470
And then this is key.

00:10:32.470 --> 00:10:35.307
When you're changing
your settings in Gmail,

00:10:35.307 --> 00:10:36.640
make sure that you save changes.

00:10:39.530 --> 00:10:40.030
Awesome.

00:10:40.030 --> 00:10:42.040
So now you can see
that that conversation

00:10:42.040 --> 00:10:46.730
bundle has been debundled and
now it's two separate emails.

00:10:46.730 --> 00:10:48.910
So if you're not a fan
of Conversation View,

00:10:48.910 --> 00:10:52.670
you're welcome, I just
totally changed your life.

00:10:52.670 --> 00:10:54.820
The next thing that I
like to show customers

00:10:54.820 --> 00:10:58.720
is something called
the Preview Pane.

00:10:58.720 --> 00:11:00.910
So a lot of people have
lived in other mail clients

00:11:00.910 --> 00:11:02.410
where they have
the ability to see

00:11:02.410 --> 00:11:04.690
their inbox on one
side and a preview

00:11:04.690 --> 00:11:06.730
of the email on the other side.

00:11:06.730 --> 00:11:09.676
So we can do that in Gmail too.

00:11:09.676 --> 00:11:12.940
If we come to Settings, we're
going to go to a new tab now,

00:11:12.940 --> 00:11:14.920
and this tab is called the Labs.

00:11:14.920 --> 00:11:18.899
Labs are experimental features
that you can enable for Gmail.

00:11:18.899 --> 00:11:20.440
These labs are going
to be controlled

00:11:20.440 --> 00:11:21.820
by your administrator.

00:11:21.820 --> 00:11:23.830
So not all of them that
I'm showing you here

00:11:23.830 --> 00:11:25.820
may be available.

00:11:25.820 --> 00:11:28.917
But that's going to be up
to your admin to figure out.

00:11:28.917 --> 00:11:30.750
There's a lot of really
great ones in here--

00:11:30.750 --> 00:11:34.750
canned responses, custom
keyboard shortcuts.

00:11:34.750 --> 00:11:38.230
But the one that I want to
show you is the Preview Pane.

00:11:38.230 --> 00:11:40.000
Again, click on Enable.

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:42.040
Don't forget to
save your changes.

00:11:42.040 --> 00:11:45.880
And it's going to
refresh our inbox for us.

00:11:45.880 --> 00:11:48.470
It looks the same right
now, but notice we

00:11:48.470 --> 00:11:49.820
have this new button up here.

00:11:49.820 --> 00:11:52.010
This is where we
control our preview.

00:11:52.010 --> 00:11:54.650
So we can choose what type
of split we want to use.

00:11:54.650 --> 00:11:56.710
I tend to like the
vertical split.

00:11:56.710 --> 00:12:00.590
And now we have the ability
to preview these messages

00:12:00.590 --> 00:12:02.000
without losing our inbox.

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:05.420
You can simply
click on your email

00:12:05.420 --> 00:12:08.060
and there it is on
the right side panel.

00:12:08.060 --> 00:12:10.430
Pretty good stuff, right?

00:12:10.430 --> 00:12:12.230
The last thing that
I like to show people

00:12:12.230 --> 00:12:15.980
in terms of physically
organizing your inbox

00:12:15.980 --> 00:12:19.340
is what I call Priority Inbox.

00:12:19.340 --> 00:12:26.060
So if we come back to our
settings, within Inbox,

00:12:26.060 --> 00:12:28.160
there's something
called an Inbox Type.

00:12:28.160 --> 00:12:33.410
Default Inbox Type is simply
where your inbox will organize

00:12:33.410 --> 00:12:34.680
things in chronological order.

00:12:34.680 --> 00:12:38.150
The most recent email
that comes in sits on top.

00:12:38.150 --> 00:12:40.490
But there's other ways
that you can do that.

00:12:40.490 --> 00:12:43.380
You can do Important first
where important emails surface

00:12:43.380 --> 00:12:43.880
to the top.

00:12:43.880 --> 00:12:46.255
You can do Unread first where
any emails that you haven't

00:12:46.255 --> 00:12:48.590
read yet surface to the top.

00:12:48.590 --> 00:12:51.710
Starred First-- similarly,
anything that has a star.

00:12:51.710 --> 00:12:53.690
I like to do what's
called the Priority Inbox.

00:12:53.690 --> 00:12:55.580
This is how I use
my personal Gmail

00:12:55.580 --> 00:12:59.030
and how I use my
work G Suite account.

00:12:59.030 --> 00:13:02.570
Priority Inbox lets you have
multiple sections as well

00:13:02.570 --> 00:13:08.240
as the ability to change and
prioritize those as needed.

00:13:08.240 --> 00:13:12.620
So first will come important and
unread, then will come starred.

00:13:12.620 --> 00:13:14.377
We can even add
labels in here too.

00:13:14.377 --> 00:13:15.710
So we're going to add a section.

00:13:15.710 --> 00:13:20.090
If we come into More Options,
here's our Rock and Ice,

00:13:20.090 --> 00:13:23.740
and then everything else.

00:13:23.740 --> 00:13:27.200
Again, we'll save our changes.

00:13:27.200 --> 00:13:30.350
And notice that things
have changed a little bit.

00:13:30.350 --> 00:13:32.470
Up here is Important and Unread.

00:13:32.470 --> 00:13:34.650
A quick note about
importance-- importance

00:13:34.650 --> 00:13:37.780
is a concept in Gmail that
is a learning algorithm.

00:13:37.780 --> 00:13:40.870
As you mark things
important or not important,

00:13:40.870 --> 00:13:43.180
based off of things
like who sent

00:13:43.180 --> 00:13:45.250
the email, the
content of the email,

00:13:45.250 --> 00:13:47.950
who's receiving the email,
it will automatically

00:13:47.950 --> 00:13:51.290
determine what you might
think is important.

00:13:51.290 --> 00:13:54.070
So you can train your algorithm
by marking things on and off

00:13:54.070 --> 00:13:56.190
and it will get
better over time.

00:13:56.190 --> 00:13:59.220
Here-- down here is what
we have for starred,

00:13:59.220 --> 00:14:01.170
then we've got our Rock and Ice.

00:14:01.170 --> 00:14:04.510
And you can collapse
these as needed.

00:14:04.510 --> 00:14:06.840
And here is everything else.

00:14:06.840 --> 00:14:08.550
The Priority Inbox
is my favorite way

00:14:08.550 --> 00:14:09.540
to manage my inbox.

00:14:09.540 --> 00:14:11.969
It gives me the ability
to surface the things that

00:14:11.969 --> 00:14:13.260
are important and unread first.

00:14:13.260 --> 00:14:17.130
Then I use stars as
similar to a to-do list.

00:14:17.130 --> 00:14:18.810
And then everything
else is stuff

00:14:18.810 --> 00:14:22.710
that I don't think is really
that important anymore.

00:14:22.710 --> 00:14:23.900
Excellent.

00:14:23.900 --> 00:14:28.580
Now, couple more things
to show you in Gmail--

00:14:28.580 --> 00:14:32.810
the first one is actually my
favorite setting in Gmail.

00:14:32.810 --> 00:14:34.490
It's called Undo Send.

00:14:34.490 --> 00:14:36.470
Let's say we're going
to compose a message

00:14:36.470 --> 00:14:39.410
and we're sending a
message to Albert Einstein,

00:14:39.410 --> 00:14:43.310
because I work with him,
and we're going to say,

00:14:43.310 --> 00:14:46.378
"Please review
this information."

00:14:49.130 --> 00:14:49.970
I'm really excited.

00:14:49.970 --> 00:14:51.800
I'm sending Albert
Einstein some info,

00:14:51.800 --> 00:14:55.080
so I'm going to say, "Hi, Bert.

00:14:55.080 --> 00:15:00.540
Please take a look below
and give me your thoughts."

00:15:00.540 --> 00:15:03.300
But what happens when
I get too excited

00:15:03.300 --> 00:15:06.720
and I accidentally just send
it without finishing the email?

00:15:06.720 --> 00:15:08.150
Have you guys ever done that?

00:15:08.150 --> 00:15:11.340
You hit Send and,
uh oh what do I do?

00:15:11.340 --> 00:15:13.770
I've actually got this
Undo Send button right here

00:15:13.770 --> 00:15:15.390
where I can click Undo.

00:15:15.390 --> 00:15:17.700
And I get my email back
and I have the ability

00:15:17.700 --> 00:15:20.790
to finish it, slow down a
little bit, proofread it,

00:15:20.790 --> 00:15:22.770
and then I can
send it out again.

00:15:22.770 --> 00:15:28.170
In order to turn on Undo
Send, you come into Settings.

00:15:28.170 --> 00:15:30.660
Under the General tab,
about four or five

00:15:30.660 --> 00:15:32.860
down, we've got Undo Send.

00:15:32.860 --> 00:15:36.570
You enable that and you get to
choose the cancellation period.

00:15:36.570 --> 00:15:39.240
I choose 30 seconds
because sometimes I

00:15:39.240 --> 00:15:40.934
get a little bit
confused and don't

00:15:40.934 --> 00:15:42.100
realize that I messed it up.

00:15:42.100 --> 00:15:45.510
But this is one of my
favorite, favorite settings,

00:15:45.510 --> 00:15:48.842
and it has saved me more
times than I can count.

00:15:48.842 --> 00:15:50.550
The last thing that
I'm going to show you

00:15:50.550 --> 00:15:54.940
about Gmail before we move on
is what we call Gmail shortcuts.

00:15:54.940 --> 00:15:56.730
So like I said, a
lot of you probably

00:15:56.730 --> 00:15:58.830
want to be Gmail power users.

00:15:58.830 --> 00:16:01.210
If you hit Shift
and question mark,

00:16:01.210 --> 00:16:04.050
it's going to pull up all
the different shortcuts

00:16:04.050 --> 00:16:07.440
that you can use to interact
with the Gmail interface.

00:16:07.440 --> 00:16:09.570
The idea behind this
is the less time

00:16:09.570 --> 00:16:11.820
you spend grabbing for your
mouse and looking around

00:16:11.820 --> 00:16:15.240
and things like that, the less
context switching that you do,

00:16:15.240 --> 00:16:18.770
the faster you're going
to be at composing emails,

00:16:18.770 --> 00:16:20.790
at being productive, right?

00:16:20.790 --> 00:16:22.140
So there's a lot of them there.

00:16:22.140 --> 00:16:23.640
You'll learn them over time.

00:16:23.640 --> 00:16:26.730
But for example, if
we want to take a look

00:16:26.730 --> 00:16:30.690
at our starred messages,
we can have simply type GS

00:16:30.690 --> 00:16:33.390
and it will automatically
pull up our starred messages.

00:16:33.390 --> 00:16:34.980
If we want to look
at our labels,

00:16:34.980 --> 00:16:39.450
we can type GL and choose
our Rock and Ice label.

00:16:39.450 --> 00:16:40.422
And here we are.

00:16:40.422 --> 00:16:41.880
If we want to go
back to our inbox,

00:16:41.880 --> 00:16:45.600
we can type G and I.
There's our inbox.

00:16:45.600 --> 00:16:47.880
And then if you wanted
to compose a new message,

00:16:47.880 --> 00:16:51.870
you simply type D, and there it
does-- it automatically brings

00:16:51.870 --> 00:16:53.820
up a new message for you.

00:16:53.820 --> 00:16:56.250
Again, the idea is we're
going to take seconds off

00:16:56.250 --> 00:16:58.230
of each task so that
those seconds add up

00:16:58.230 --> 00:17:00.000
to minutes throughout
the week, which

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:04.710
adds up to hours throughout
the months, so on and so forth.

00:17:04.710 --> 00:17:05.210
Awesome.

00:17:05.210 --> 00:17:07.310
So to review, in Gmail,
we've learned a little bit

00:17:07.310 --> 00:17:09.650
about searching,
both advanced as well

00:17:09.650 --> 00:17:11.869
as super advanced with
the search operators.

00:17:11.869 --> 00:17:14.300
We've learned about labels,
we've learned about filtering,

00:17:14.300 --> 00:17:16.508
and then we've learned about
how to visually organize

00:17:16.508 --> 00:17:18.660
your inbox in different ways.

00:17:18.660 --> 00:17:20.660
But I don't know about you.

00:17:20.660 --> 00:17:23.400
My life is controlled by my
calendar, not by my inbox.

00:17:23.400 --> 00:17:27.109
So if it doesn't exist on
my calendar, it's not real.

00:17:27.109 --> 00:17:28.850
It's not in my head.

00:17:28.850 --> 00:17:30.620
So let's move over
to Calendar and look

00:17:30.620 --> 00:17:33.690
at some of the cool
things we can do here.

00:17:33.690 --> 00:17:36.080
This right here is
actually called the waffle.

00:17:36.080 --> 00:17:38.600
So you select the waffle and
this gives you the opportunity

00:17:38.600 --> 00:17:42.770
to choose a new application.

00:17:42.770 --> 00:17:45.820
For those of you that are G
Suite customers or use Gmail

00:17:45.820 --> 00:17:50.420
in your personal life, you've
probably seen the new Calendar

00:17:50.420 --> 00:17:51.410
interface.

00:17:51.410 --> 00:17:53.900
But if you haven't,
that's all right.

00:17:53.900 --> 00:17:56.960
Welcome to the new
Calendar interface.

00:17:56.960 --> 00:18:00.950
We'll start in here the same
place that we started in Gmail,

00:18:00.950 --> 00:18:02.180
and that's searching.

00:18:02.180 --> 00:18:04.040
You can, again, search by terms.

00:18:04.040 --> 00:18:07.220
But let's take a look at
the Advanced Search options.

00:18:07.220 --> 00:18:10.370
This gives you a lot
of really good ways

00:18:10.370 --> 00:18:14.170
to look through your events
that are much more powerful.

00:18:14.170 --> 00:18:16.790
So we can look at
different calendars,

00:18:16.790 --> 00:18:21.440
we can type in keywords,
we can select organizers

00:18:21.440 --> 00:18:25.160
or participants, we can
choose location or room,

00:18:25.160 --> 00:18:29.420
we can exclude keywords,
and then also the dates.

00:18:29.420 --> 00:18:31.250
Again, the idea
is to make it easy

00:18:31.250 --> 00:18:34.920
for you to find what
you're looking for.

00:18:34.920 --> 00:18:38.554
Now, the next thing I'm going to
show you is pretty interesting.

00:18:38.554 --> 00:18:40.220
I'm sure you're all
aware of how to view

00:18:40.220 --> 00:18:42.190
a calendar of your coworker.

00:18:42.190 --> 00:18:45.200
You simply come into here, you
can type in your coworker's

00:18:45.200 --> 00:18:49.250
name, select it, and now we
can see that person's calendar

00:18:49.250 --> 00:18:50.720
overlaid with mine.

00:18:50.720 --> 00:18:53.000
But there's something
called Public Calendars

00:18:53.000 --> 00:18:54.560
that are pretty interesting.

00:18:54.560 --> 00:18:57.900
If you click on the plus
and Add Other Calendars,

00:18:57.900 --> 00:18:59.490
you can browse
calendars of interest.

00:18:59.490 --> 00:19:01.430
This is going to
give us a number

00:19:01.430 --> 00:19:04.370
of religious and
regional holidays,

00:19:04.370 --> 00:19:07.010
a very long list of
different regional holidays.

00:19:07.010 --> 00:19:09.860
So if you're working
with a particular region

00:19:09.860 --> 00:19:13.670
very frequently, you can be
up to date on their holidays.

00:19:13.670 --> 00:19:16.490
If you're a sports fan, it also
has public sports calendars.

00:19:16.490 --> 00:19:20.030
I used to live in Chicago,
so we can come into Hockey,

00:19:20.030 --> 00:19:23.930
take a look at the NHL,
select the Blackhawks,

00:19:23.930 --> 00:19:26.060
and we've added
that calendar now.

00:19:26.060 --> 00:19:27.870
I can scroll down
and I can see here

00:19:27.870 --> 00:19:30.020
are all the Blackhawks events.

00:19:30.020 --> 00:19:32.420
Pretty cool, right?

00:19:32.420 --> 00:19:33.230
Awesome.

00:19:33.230 --> 00:19:35.330
The next thing that
I'd like to show you

00:19:35.330 --> 00:19:38.480
is what's called
appointment slots.

00:19:38.480 --> 00:19:40.850
Imagine tomorrow I'm
going to be meeting

00:19:40.850 --> 00:19:43.250
with a large number of
customers-- six, seven,

00:19:43.250 --> 00:19:44.420
eight customers.

00:19:44.420 --> 00:19:47.320
And instead of emailing each
one individually and trying

00:19:47.320 --> 00:19:49.910
to coordinate all the
different times that

00:19:49.910 --> 00:19:51.680
are possible for me
to meet with them,

00:19:51.680 --> 00:19:53.760
I'm going to create
an appointment slot,

00:19:53.760 --> 00:19:56.990
send everybody one link,
and let them determine

00:19:56.990 --> 00:19:58.470
when there is availability.

00:19:58.470 --> 00:20:02.210
So to do that, we're
going to drag and drop.

00:20:02.210 --> 00:20:05.960
We'll do our appointments
tomorrow from 8:00 to noon.

00:20:05.960 --> 00:20:10.420
We're going to call this
"Meetings with Kurt."

00:20:10.420 --> 00:20:14.416
And up here, we choose
Appointment Slots.

00:20:14.416 --> 00:20:15.790
The next thing
that we have to do

00:20:15.790 --> 00:20:17.740
is determine what our
duration is going to be.

00:20:17.740 --> 00:20:20.650
We're going to make
30-minute meetings.

00:20:20.650 --> 00:20:23.110
And we'll save this.

00:20:23.110 --> 00:20:25.282
Now, instead of me
having to coordinate

00:20:25.282 --> 00:20:26.740
with all these
people, I can simply

00:20:26.740 --> 00:20:29.920
click on this Calendar's
Appointment Page,

00:20:29.920 --> 00:20:34.450
copy the link, and then send
that link to a customer.

00:20:34.450 --> 00:20:36.640
If we were to-- this
is another environment

00:20:36.640 --> 00:20:39.460
that I have-- if we were
to put this in and give it

00:20:39.460 --> 00:20:42.340
to the customer, it's actually
going to show a calendar

00:20:42.340 --> 00:20:45.740
interface where it overlays
their calendar with all

00:20:45.740 --> 00:20:47.280
of these open appointments.

00:20:47.280 --> 00:20:49.750
And in order for that person
to schedule a meeting with me,

00:20:49.750 --> 00:20:54.680
they simply have to click on
one, fill out the information,

00:20:54.680 --> 00:20:57.269
and save it.

00:20:57.269 --> 00:20:58.560
That appointment's been booked.

00:20:58.560 --> 00:21:01.530
And if we go and look back
at my original calendar,

00:21:01.530 --> 00:21:04.260
we should have a new
appointment right here

00:21:04.260 --> 00:21:05.460
that we just scheduled.

00:21:05.460 --> 00:21:08.120
A really simple and
easy way for you

00:21:08.120 --> 00:21:10.140
to minimize the amount
of time that you're

00:21:10.140 --> 00:21:14.010
doing coordinating and shuffling
times and all of that--

00:21:14.010 --> 00:21:17.864
just simply with
calendar appointments.

00:21:17.864 --> 00:21:19.530
Next couple of things
I want to show you

00:21:19.530 --> 00:21:22.180
are small, but powerful things.

00:21:22.180 --> 00:21:24.060
One is within the
new Calendar, you

00:21:24.060 --> 00:21:25.980
have the ability to
restore deleted events

00:21:25.980 --> 00:21:27.910
in a very easy way.

00:21:27.910 --> 00:21:32.370
Let's say I've got a
proposal meeting tomorrow

00:21:32.370 --> 00:21:35.250
and I accidentally delete it
thinking it was something else.

00:21:35.250 --> 00:21:39.990
Not a problem-- you simply come
up to the Settings, into Trash,

00:21:39.990 --> 00:21:44.930
and here I can select
and restore this event.

00:21:44.930 --> 00:21:48.540
And if we come back,
in just a moment

00:21:48.540 --> 00:21:52.300
it should be restored--
nice and simple.

00:21:52.300 --> 00:21:54.660
The next one is what I
call invisible events.

00:21:54.660 --> 00:21:57.432
Imagine on Thursday
morning I'm going

00:21:57.432 --> 00:21:58.890
to go rock climbing
for the morning

00:21:58.890 --> 00:22:01.540
instead of coming
into the office.

00:22:01.540 --> 00:22:04.230
I don't want my boss to know
that I'm out rock climbing,

00:22:04.230 --> 00:22:07.361
but I also don't want him to see
that "Do Not Schedule" block,

00:22:07.361 --> 00:22:07.860
right?

00:22:07.860 --> 00:22:11.920
So we'll call this
"Rock Climbing."

00:22:11.920 --> 00:22:13.850
And we're going to
come into More Options.

00:22:13.850 --> 00:22:16.060
If I mark it as
Private, it'll still

00:22:16.060 --> 00:22:18.290
show up on my
calendar as an event,

00:22:18.290 --> 00:22:20.890
but nobody can see the details.

00:22:20.890 --> 00:22:24.670
But if I mark it as Private
and I change my status to Free,

00:22:24.670 --> 00:22:27.672
then it won't show up on
anybody else's calendar,

00:22:27.672 --> 00:22:29.380
and I'll be the only
one that can see it,

00:22:29.380 --> 00:22:33.390
effectively making it
an invisible event.

00:22:33.390 --> 00:22:36.030
Pretty good stuff there.

00:22:36.030 --> 00:22:38.940
Last two things are new
settings in Calendar

00:22:38.940 --> 00:22:42.030
that I think are
worth taking note of.

00:22:42.030 --> 00:22:45.240
On the left here, we have
our time for our schedule.

00:22:45.240 --> 00:22:48.250
You have the ability to have
a second time zone displayed.

00:22:48.250 --> 00:22:52.270
So if we come into our
Settings, under Time Zones,

00:22:52.270 --> 00:22:54.370
you can display a
second time zone.

00:22:54.370 --> 00:22:56.950
I'm frequently working
with Mountain View where

00:22:56.950 --> 00:22:58.570
Google's headquarters
is, so I'm going

00:22:58.570 --> 00:23:01.240
to have that be my
second time zone.

00:23:01.240 --> 00:23:03.520
Now we come back
and automatically,

00:23:03.520 --> 00:23:06.010
as I'm scheduling things,
as I'm building out

00:23:06.010 --> 00:23:08.770
my calendar for the
week, I can easily

00:23:08.770 --> 00:23:13.300
see what the time
is in Mountain View.

00:23:13.300 --> 00:23:15.430
And then another thing
that's really great

00:23:15.430 --> 00:23:16.900
is what's called working hours.

00:23:16.900 --> 00:23:19.930
So I'm very protective
of my work-life balance.

00:23:19.930 --> 00:23:24.700
So within our Settings,
I've set working hours such

00:23:24.700 --> 00:23:31.120
that when people try to invite
me from Monday through Friday

00:23:31.120 --> 00:23:33.772
outside of the hours
from 9:00 to 6:00 PM,

00:23:33.772 --> 00:23:35.230
they'll actually
receive a warning,

00:23:35.230 --> 00:23:37.090
and that warning will
say, "Are you sure

00:23:37.090 --> 00:23:38.680
you want to schedule
it at this time?

00:23:38.680 --> 00:23:41.790
This is outside of
Kurt's working hours."

00:23:41.790 --> 00:23:42.900
Pretty good stuff.

00:23:42.900 --> 00:23:44.640
Some of you may
not have the luxury

00:23:44.640 --> 00:23:47.140
of being able to schedule
those working hours.

00:23:47.140 --> 00:23:50.520
But if you do, I
highly recommend it,

00:23:50.520 --> 00:23:52.950
although what I've found
is that it hasn't really

00:23:52.950 --> 00:23:55.780
kept anybody from scheduling
outside of those hours anyway.

00:23:55.780 --> 00:23:57.210
Oh well.

00:23:57.210 --> 00:24:01.020
Last thing in Google
Calendar-- similar to how

00:24:01.020 --> 00:24:05.040
we have shortcuts in Gmail,
we have a large number

00:24:05.040 --> 00:24:07.290
of shortcuts in Google Calendar.

00:24:07.290 --> 00:24:10.080
And so with the
press of a button,

00:24:10.080 --> 00:24:11.820
you can do things
like change the view,

00:24:11.820 --> 00:24:14.910
if we would rather see
a day view versus a week

00:24:14.910 --> 00:24:17.640
view versus a month view.

00:24:17.640 --> 00:24:19.750
You also have a custom
view, which in this case

00:24:19.750 --> 00:24:21.770
I've set to three days.

00:24:21.770 --> 00:24:23.877
And you can also
select on the side

00:24:23.877 --> 00:24:25.210
and choose your own custom view.

00:24:25.210 --> 00:24:27.720
If I wanted to do
a four-day view,

00:24:27.720 --> 00:24:30.390
you can select on this
calendar the four-day view,

00:24:30.390 --> 00:24:32.640
and then it will maintain
that as you scroll

00:24:32.640 --> 00:24:35.490
through different time periods.

00:24:35.490 --> 00:24:36.554
Excellent.

00:24:36.554 --> 00:24:37.970
We've talked a lot
about Calendar.

00:24:37.970 --> 00:24:39.960
We've talked about Gmail.

00:24:39.960 --> 00:24:42.785
Let's spend a few minutes
in Google Drive and Docs.

00:24:47.260 --> 00:24:53.000
So within Google Drive, there's
a couple of interesting things

00:24:53.000 --> 00:24:53.750
to show you.

00:24:53.750 --> 00:24:55.710
Again, we have an
Advanced Search feature,

00:24:55.710 --> 00:24:58.160
which gives you a lot
of really robust ways

00:24:58.160 --> 00:25:01.040
to search through your data.

00:25:01.040 --> 00:25:03.980
The next thing is an
extension that I think

00:25:03.980 --> 00:25:06.090
everybody should be using.

00:25:06.090 --> 00:25:11.270
It's called the Office
Editing for Docs extension.

00:25:11.270 --> 00:25:14.690
If you search for that
and pull it up in Chrome,

00:25:14.690 --> 00:25:18.140
this gives you the ability
to edit Office docs

00:25:18.140 --> 00:25:22.274
in their native format
while in Google--

00:25:22.274 --> 00:25:24.440
really highly recommend it
because we recognize here

00:25:24.440 --> 00:25:26.570
at Google that
oftentimes you're not

00:25:26.570 --> 00:25:28.700
going to be working with
somebody else in Google's

00:25:28.700 --> 00:25:30.950
environment, and so you
need to have the ability

00:25:30.950 --> 00:25:32.230
to edit those documents.

00:25:34.860 --> 00:25:43.730
If we take a look at
a document that is not

00:25:43.730 --> 00:25:45.410
a Google formatted
document, you also

00:25:45.410 --> 00:25:48.641
have the ability to
do versioning as well.

00:25:48.641 --> 00:25:49.640
So within a Google doc--

00:25:49.640 --> 00:25:52.014
I'm sure you're aware-- you
can click on Revision History

00:25:52.014 --> 00:25:53.730
and see every change
that you've made.

00:25:53.730 --> 00:25:57.920
You can do something similar
to that within Google Drive.

00:25:57.920 --> 00:26:00.770
So if you select-- this
is a Word document here--

00:26:00.770 --> 00:26:03.880
if you select that and
you click on View Details,

00:26:03.880 --> 00:26:07.190
you come into Details down
here in the bottom right,

00:26:07.190 --> 00:26:09.092
it gives you the ability
to manage versions.

00:26:09.092 --> 00:26:11.300
We can see all the different
versions that we've had.

00:26:11.300 --> 00:26:13.181
I edited it at 11:11.

00:26:13.181 --> 00:26:14.930
And you can see all
the different versions

00:26:14.930 --> 00:26:18.140
all the way back to when
it was created in 2014.

00:26:18.140 --> 00:26:19.850
This is an old one.

00:26:19.850 --> 00:26:21.840
You can upload new versions.

00:26:21.840 --> 00:26:26.025
You can also download or delete
particular versions, something

00:26:26.025 --> 00:26:28.400
that a lot of people don't
recognize when they're dealing

00:26:28.400 --> 00:26:30.050
with other formatted types.

00:26:33.099 --> 00:26:34.640
Within Google Drive,
as well, there's

00:26:34.640 --> 00:26:36.590
something called Quick Access.

00:26:36.590 --> 00:26:39.950
So Quick Access is a
setting that you can turn on

00:26:39.950 --> 00:26:44.030
under Suggestions, which will
actually take into account when

00:26:44.030 --> 00:26:46.910
you last opened a document, what
things are on your schedule,

00:26:46.910 --> 00:26:51.320
and it will automatically
surface the things that it

00:26:51.320 --> 00:26:56.440
thinks you're going to
work on most often, which

00:26:56.440 --> 00:26:59.850
is a great feature as well.

00:26:59.850 --> 00:27:00.350
Excellent.

00:27:00.350 --> 00:27:02.676
We've talked a little
bit about versioning.

00:27:02.676 --> 00:27:05.300
We've talked a little bit about
how to edit Microsoft documents

00:27:05.300 --> 00:27:05.870
in Drive.

00:27:05.870 --> 00:27:09.840
Next, let's step
into a Google Sheet

00:27:09.840 --> 00:27:11.840
and show you some of the
really cool things that

00:27:11.840 --> 00:27:13.920
are coming out for that.

00:27:13.920 --> 00:27:20.380
So if we come into our folder
here, I have a new sheet.

00:27:20.380 --> 00:27:22.490
It's called Black
Friday Week Sales Data.

00:27:22.490 --> 00:27:24.710
So it's from this
past Black Friday.

00:27:24.710 --> 00:27:27.790
And what I want to do
is-- well, first of all,

00:27:27.790 --> 00:27:30.880
it's a little bit hard to see.

00:27:30.880 --> 00:27:32.410
It's not formatted well.

00:27:32.410 --> 00:27:34.870
So let's do a little
bit of quick formatting

00:27:34.870 --> 00:27:36.250
to make it easier to see.

00:27:36.250 --> 00:27:38.160
The first thing that
we're going to do--

00:27:38.160 --> 00:27:40.300
we'll select everything.

00:27:40.300 --> 00:27:43.180
If we go into Format,
Alternating Colors,

00:27:43.180 --> 00:27:45.790
this is a really
quick and easy way

00:27:45.790 --> 00:27:50.470
to just add some
color to the document

00:27:50.470 --> 00:27:53.110
to make it easier to read.

00:27:53.110 --> 00:27:55.030
We can also do something
called freezing.

00:27:55.030 --> 00:27:58.360
So if we go into View,
we freeze our first row,

00:27:58.360 --> 00:28:01.690
and now, as we scroll
down, the title row

00:28:01.690 --> 00:28:04.270
stays with it to
make it easier to see

00:28:04.270 --> 00:28:06.670
what all these numbers are.

00:28:06.670 --> 00:28:07.760
That's all pretty awesome.

00:28:07.760 --> 00:28:09.850
But what I really want
to do is get some insight

00:28:09.850 --> 00:28:10.840
from this data.

00:28:10.840 --> 00:28:14.340
We have this new
feature called Explore.

00:28:14.340 --> 00:28:17.680
The Explore feature
will give us the ability

00:28:17.680 --> 00:28:19.270
to do some really
interesting things.

00:28:19.270 --> 00:28:21.765
It's powered by some awesome AI.

00:28:21.765 --> 00:28:23.140
And the first
thing you'll notice

00:28:23.140 --> 00:28:27.730
is that it gives us an automatic
suggestion for a pivot table.

00:28:27.730 --> 00:28:30.160
In this case, the pivot
table displays average

00:28:30.160 --> 00:28:32.530
of unit price for each item.

00:28:32.530 --> 00:28:35.140
Really useful-- if you had
to build that pivot table

00:28:35.140 --> 00:28:36.880
on your own, it might
take you some time.

00:28:36.880 --> 00:28:39.280
You can simply just
click and add that in.

00:28:39.280 --> 00:28:41.860
It also does some
initial analysis--

00:28:41.860 --> 00:28:44.510
in this case, cost of
goods versus profit.

00:28:44.510 --> 00:28:46.450
And you can scroll
through and see which

00:28:46.450 --> 00:28:47.950
ones you find interesting.

00:28:47.950 --> 00:28:51.070
And then in order to add it,
you simply click Insert Chart,

00:28:51.070 --> 00:28:52.180
and there we go.

00:28:52.180 --> 00:28:56.050
With a click of a button, we've
got a Count of Items graph

00:28:56.050 --> 00:28:56.770
here--

00:28:56.770 --> 00:28:57.980
pretty useful.

00:28:57.980 --> 00:29:00.340
But let's say we
want even more data.

00:29:00.340 --> 00:29:02.590
We want to learn
even more about it.

00:29:02.590 --> 00:29:05.410
If we come back to
our Explore function,

00:29:05.410 --> 00:29:08.680
we can actually ask
natural language questions

00:29:08.680 --> 00:29:10.780
of this feature and
get answers back.

00:29:10.780 --> 00:29:13.180
So let's say we want
to know, what item

00:29:13.180 --> 00:29:17.650
has the highest profit margin?

00:29:17.650 --> 00:29:20.380
It actually understands
that question as top item

00:29:20.380 --> 00:29:22.330
by profit margin,
which in this case

00:29:22.330 --> 00:29:26.500
was the watch with a
profit margin of 0.6.

00:29:26.500 --> 00:29:29.680
And we can click in even
further and see the query

00:29:29.680 --> 00:29:32.410
that it used to
figure that out, which

00:29:32.410 --> 00:29:36.070
I'm pretty decent with Google
Sheets, but would have taken me

00:29:36.070 --> 00:29:39.152
quite a while to
figure that out.

00:29:39.152 --> 00:29:42.400
Awesome.

00:29:42.400 --> 00:29:44.920
Another really interesting
thing about Google Sheets

00:29:44.920 --> 00:29:47.710
is you have the ability to turn
on notifications in the event

00:29:47.710 --> 00:29:49.880
that somebody
edits the document.

00:29:49.880 --> 00:29:57.100
So if we come in to
Tools, Notification Rules,

00:29:57.100 --> 00:30:01.450
you can choose notify me at
my email address any time

00:30:01.450 --> 00:30:03.940
changes are made, and
you can do it either

00:30:03.940 --> 00:30:08.350
on a daily digest
or email right away.

00:30:08.350 --> 00:30:09.080
We click Save.

00:30:09.080 --> 00:30:11.830
And now any time somebody
adds data to this,

00:30:11.830 --> 00:30:16.480
I'll be able to recognize
it and see the new data--

00:30:16.480 --> 00:30:17.390
pretty good stuff.

00:30:17.390 --> 00:30:20.782
So let's review all
that we've seen today.

00:30:20.782 --> 00:30:21.740
We've seen quite a bit.

00:30:21.740 --> 00:30:23.140
We've talked about Gmail.

00:30:23.140 --> 00:30:26.591
We've talked about how to
search and organize your inbox.

00:30:26.591 --> 00:30:28.090
We've talked about
Calendar and some

00:30:28.090 --> 00:30:30.370
of the cool tricks
with the new Calendar

00:30:30.370 --> 00:30:33.280
in order to manage your
days and times, especially

00:30:33.280 --> 00:30:34.805
appointment slots.

00:30:34.805 --> 00:30:36.430
We've talked about
Google Drive and how

00:30:36.430 --> 00:30:38.980
to work with some files
that aren't Google

00:30:38.980 --> 00:30:41.770
file types in Google Drive,
both through versioning

00:30:41.770 --> 00:30:45.604
as well as through
the extension.

00:30:45.604 --> 00:30:47.020
And then we've
talked a little bit

00:30:47.020 --> 00:30:49.750
about Google Sheets and some of
the excellent new features that

00:30:49.750 --> 00:30:54.850
are coming into Google Sheets
around Explore and formatting

00:30:54.850 --> 00:30:56.330
and things along those lines.

00:30:56.330 --> 00:30:59.710
So that's everything that I
wanted to show you for the demo

00:30:59.710 --> 00:31:00.430
today.

00:31:00.430 --> 00:31:03.235
We're going to take
some live questions.

00:31:03.235 --> 00:31:05.860
So stay tuned for that and we'll
be back in less than a minute.

00:34:22.639 --> 00:34:23.139
All right.

00:34:23.139 --> 00:34:24.370
Welcome back, everyone.

00:34:24.370 --> 00:34:25.199
Thanks for sticking with us.

00:34:25.199 --> 00:34:27.157
We're going to go through
a couple of questions

00:34:27.157 --> 00:34:27.750
that came up.

00:34:27.750 --> 00:34:29.880
Before I get
started on those, we

00:34:29.880 --> 00:34:32.250
noticed that quite a few of
the questions that came in

00:34:32.250 --> 00:34:34.039
were about product roadmap.

00:34:34.039 --> 00:34:36.330
I just want to give you all
a little bit of a heads up.

00:34:36.330 --> 00:34:38.190
If you are asking
about product roadmap

00:34:38.190 --> 00:34:39.840
and you're current
customer, please

00:34:39.840 --> 00:34:42.989
feel free to reach out to your
Google account manager and they

00:34:42.989 --> 00:34:46.380
can help you walk through the
product roadmap for both G

00:34:46.380 --> 00:34:47.469
Suite and Google Cloud.

00:34:47.469 --> 00:34:49.260
If you're a prospective
customer and you're

00:34:49.260 --> 00:34:51.840
interested in learning more
about our product roadmap,

00:34:51.840 --> 00:34:53.820
please go and visit
the Google Cloud

00:34:53.820 --> 00:34:56.880
blog where we'll make
all public announcements.

00:34:56.880 --> 00:34:57.780
Awesome.

00:34:57.780 --> 00:35:01.080
So we've got five questions
here that we can potentially

00:35:01.080 --> 00:35:02.590
address today.

00:35:02.590 --> 00:35:06.570
The first is whether the company
is the ultimate owner of data

00:35:06.570 --> 00:35:08.280
for every user in G Suite--

00:35:08.280 --> 00:35:10.410
that's absolutely correct.

00:35:10.410 --> 00:35:12.870
When you're putting
your data into G Suite,

00:35:12.870 --> 00:35:14.700
Google identifies
that you are the owner

00:35:14.700 --> 00:35:16.110
of that data and
Google is what's

00:35:16.110 --> 00:35:19.410
called a data processor,
meaning that at any time

00:35:19.410 --> 00:35:22.260
you have the ability to
remove that data from Google.

00:35:22.260 --> 00:35:24.060
We don't use that data
for advertisements.

00:35:24.060 --> 00:35:26.520
We don't use that data for
improving our products.

00:35:26.520 --> 00:35:28.580
We don't use that
data for selling.

00:35:28.580 --> 00:35:29.850
We don't do anything with it.

00:35:29.850 --> 00:35:31.560
It's your data.

00:35:31.560 --> 00:35:33.540
But thanks for asking.

00:35:33.540 --> 00:35:35.580
Regarding Google
Vault-- if I enable

00:35:35.580 --> 00:35:38.250
Vault for an organization
and set the retention period

00:35:38.250 --> 00:35:40.950
to undefined, if an
employee leaves the company

00:35:40.950 --> 00:35:43.770
and I delete his or her
account, can I browse and export

00:35:43.770 --> 00:35:45.870
a specific file of this user?

00:35:45.870 --> 00:35:48.150
So this is an
interesting question.

00:35:48.150 --> 00:35:50.820
For undefined, I
think you probably

00:35:50.820 --> 00:35:53.490
meant indefinite,
which means you

00:35:53.490 --> 00:35:55.410
would keep that data forever.

00:35:55.410 --> 00:35:59.520
But there's a
particular caveat here.

00:35:59.520 --> 00:36:01.380
When you're using
Google Vault and you

00:36:01.380 --> 00:36:05.010
have a user that
stored data there,

00:36:05.010 --> 00:36:07.080
whether it's in a legal
hold or whether it's

00:36:07.080 --> 00:36:10.320
in just the normal
retention period, if you

00:36:10.320 --> 00:36:14.220
delete that user, that
data is no longer around.

00:36:14.220 --> 00:36:15.980
And so what you
want to do-- there's

00:36:15.980 --> 00:36:17.230
a couple of different options.

00:36:17.230 --> 00:36:19.860
If you're going to
be deleting a user

00:36:19.860 --> 00:36:21.260
and you want to
save that data, I

00:36:21.260 --> 00:36:24.825
would recommend doing the export
prior to deleting the user.

00:36:24.825 --> 00:36:26.200
There are a couple
other options.

00:36:26.200 --> 00:36:31.651
And if this is an important
part of the platform for you,

00:36:31.651 --> 00:36:33.900
please reach out to your
Google account manager and we

00:36:33.900 --> 00:36:37.590
can talk a little bit
more about the details.

00:36:37.590 --> 00:36:38.730
All right.

00:36:38.730 --> 00:36:41.910
Can we put Google
Drive documents

00:36:41.910 --> 00:36:44.370
into labels instead of
putting them in folders?

00:36:44.370 --> 00:36:46.860
In that case, what happens
if the folders are shared?

00:36:46.860 --> 00:36:51.420
So at this point, the files
that we store in Google Drive

00:36:51.420 --> 00:36:52.650
have limited metadata.

00:36:52.650 --> 00:36:55.770
You have the ability to look
at the metadata that is there

00:36:55.770 --> 00:36:57.880
and add a small description.

00:36:57.880 --> 00:37:00.510
But the concept of
labels don't actually

00:37:00.510 --> 00:37:02.620
exist at this point
in Google Drive.

00:37:02.620 --> 00:37:04.260
So if that's
something that you're

00:37:04.260 --> 00:37:06.150
looking for in terms
of functionality,

00:37:06.150 --> 00:37:10.440
we have a number of
third-party providers

00:37:10.440 --> 00:37:13.050
that can add that
type of functionality.

00:37:13.050 --> 00:37:14.190
One would be Powertools.

00:37:14.190 --> 00:37:15.780
Another would be AODocs.

00:37:15.780 --> 00:37:18.180
Those are both
third-party platforms

00:37:18.180 --> 00:37:20.400
that are built on top
of Google Drive that

00:37:20.400 --> 00:37:24.690
help you with your sort
of content management, CMS

00:37:24.690 --> 00:37:27.400
type of needs.

00:37:27.400 --> 00:37:29.430
Can people book
appointments with Calendar

00:37:29.430 --> 00:37:32.220
Appointments if they don't
have a Google account?

00:37:32.220 --> 00:37:35.040
They can book appointments with
Calendar appointment slots.

00:37:35.040 --> 00:37:37.320
The difference being
is that it's not

00:37:37.320 --> 00:37:41.360
going to show then an
example of their calendar.

00:37:41.360 --> 00:37:43.920
It would just give them the
opportunity to book the slot,

00:37:43.920 --> 00:37:46.650
and then they would have to
manage the calendar appointment

00:37:46.650 --> 00:37:48.720
in their own calendar.

00:37:48.720 --> 00:37:51.300
Finally, the
ever-popular question,

00:37:51.300 --> 00:37:55.080
can we turn off Conversation
Mode on mobile or iOS?

00:37:55.080 --> 00:37:58.210
And at this point,
it is not possible.

00:37:58.210 --> 00:38:00.990
Unfortunately, you're stuck
with Conversation Mode

00:38:00.990 --> 00:38:03.820
in your mobile device,
unless, of course,

00:38:03.820 --> 00:38:08.380
you choose to use the native iOS
Mail application, in which case

00:38:08.380 --> 00:38:10.710
you would need to have access
from your administrator

00:38:10.710 --> 00:38:15.611
to do IMAP mapping for
your Gmail account.

00:38:15.611 --> 00:38:16.110
Excellent.

00:38:16.110 --> 00:38:19.222
Those are all the questions
that we've got for you today.

00:38:19.222 --> 00:38:20.680
Thank you very much
for joining us.

00:38:20.680 --> 00:38:22.470
Please be sure to
tune in next week

00:38:22.470 --> 00:38:24.630
for more information
about Google Cloud.

00:38:24.630 --> 00:38:26.880
Appreciate everybody's time.

